Features
────────
  No UI components, per the command line.

  Start from a preset, or answer for each feature.

  1. Lean
     The architecture, a theme skeleton and every build guard, with
     networking, image loading, your font, leak detection in debug builds,
     deep links and CI. The app opens on a blank start screen for your
     design system to build on.
  2. Standard
     The lean set plus the logic of sign-in, onboarding, settings and the
     app's own language, each behind a placeholder route, with the in-app
     browser, feature flags, the developer panel with its performance
     overlay, a local database and the reference list-detail feature to
     copy. What most products want on day one, ready for your own design
     system.
  3. Everything
     Every feature that does not need UI components, working together: the
     template's own proof that they coexist without them. Delete what you
     do not need.
  4. Choose individually
     One question per feature.

  Which [2]:   Anything you skip can be added later; each is one module or one file.

  Forced update and maintenance screens
    The server can require an update (HTTP 426, or a minimum-version header)
    or announce maintenance (503), whichever screen made the call, and the app
    covers itself with a placeholder for either: a tap opens the store listing
    for an update and retries during maintenance, until your own design
    replaces the placeholder.
    Requires: network
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Image loading (Coil)
    Coil's image loader on the app's own HTTP client, sharing its connections
    and its certificate pins, ready for the image component your design system
    draws.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  App database (Room 3)
    A Room 3 database with a table per domain, emptied when the person signs
    out. With the reference feature, its list is offline-first: the list's
    state holds what was fetched last at once, and a refresh writes the
    server's answer into the table.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  REST networking (Ktor)
    One HTTP client for the whole app: typed results, the session's token read
    for every request and refreshed one call at a time, every failure
    classified (offline, timeout, the server's own error code, an unreadable
    body), and the connection state as a hint a screen can show.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  Offline request queue
    Changes made without a connection are queued and sent when one returns, by
    a WorkManager worker that waits for the network. Refusals are kept with
    the server's answer; rate limits and timeouts are retried. WorkManager
    carries a copy of Room of its own, so this adds more code than it looks.
    Requires: network, database
    You left out database; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Paged feed
    The feed's data on Paging 3, behind its ViewModel and a placeholder route:
    pages load as the list asks for them, a failed page keeps what is already
    loaded and can be retried, and a refresh reloads from where the reader is.
    The list it pages into is yours to build.
    Requires: network
  Include? [y/N]: 
  WebSocket
    One long-lived socket that is open while the app is in front: pings, a
    backoff that grows until a frame gets through, the new token after an
    account change, and a connection state a screen can render.
    Requires: network
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Biometric app lock
    A fingerprint, face or screen-lock prompt at launch and after half a
    minute away, over a placeholder cover that asks again on a tap, and the
    app kept out of the task switcher's thumbnail while the lock is on. It
    starts off: the settings ViewModel's AppLockToggled turns it on, from the
    switch your settings screen draws.
    Requires: settings
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Sign in, sign up and password reset
    Sign-in, sign-up and password-reset ViewModels against your auth
    endpoints, behind three placeholder routes, writing the encrypted token
    store. Their form state holds each field's text and error, with the
    server's field errors mapped back onto the field that caused them, for the
    inputs your sign-in screen draws.
    Requires: network, forms
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Sign in with Google
    Credential Manager's Google sign-in, ready for the control your sign-in
    screen draws to hand its ID token to the sign-in ViewModel. The token goes
    to your backend, which answers with its own tokens like any sign-in.
    Requires: auth
    You left out auth; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Profile screen
    A name, a bio and an avatar, saved on the device per user, behind the
    profile's ViewModel and a placeholder route. With the media picker on, the
    ViewModel takes a picked photo for the avatar.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Form state and validation
    Each field's text lives in a TextFieldState the ViewModel owns, so typing
    never waits on a state update and survives process death; validators
    return error codes the screen turns into copy, and server-side errors map
    back onto the fields that caused them.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  In-app language picker
    AppLocales sets and reads the app's own language: the system's per-app
    language on Android 13+, so the choice also appears in the phone's
    settings, applied by hand on older releases. The settings ViewModel holds
    it in its state and applies a choice through its route, for the picker
    your settings screen draws.
    Requires: settings
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Open source licences screen
    The real dependency list, generated from the resolved classpath at build
    time and loaded by its ViewModel behind a placeholder route. The build
    also fails on a licence the project has not allowed.
    Requires: settings
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Onboarding flow
    An onboarding ViewModel over three pages of copy in resources, behind a
    placeholder route: finishing and skipping both record it in the preference
    store, so a person goes through it once. A first launch still opens on the
    blank start screen; AppRoot can open onboarding instead once you draw its
    pages.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Reference feature
    A list + detail feature against a live public API, with its tests: keys,
    ViewModels, repository and navigation behind two placeholder routes that
    load and open items as the finished ones will. Copy its shape for your
    first feature, then delete it.
    Requires: network
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Search screen
    Results as you type with a debounce, and recent searches kept per user,
    behind the search ViewModel and a placeholder route; its state holds the
    results, whether they are loading and why they failed.
    Requires: network
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Settings screen
    The settings ViewModel, wired to the preference store and the session:
    theme, analytics opt-out, haptics and sign-out, with the build version in
    its state, behind a placeholder route. Your settings screen draws the
    switches and asks before it sends sign-out.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  In-app browser
    Web pages open in a Custom Tab coloured like the app rather than in the
    browser, through rememberInAppBrowser() in core:ui, for the links you
    draw. With settings on, the settings module's resources hold the privacy
    and terms addresses for the links your settings screen draws.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Deep linking
    A custom scheme plus verified App Links. Each feature maps its own links
    to a destination; a link is held until start-up has read the settings and
    the session, then opens on the back stack, which survives process death,
    over the start screen, whether or not anyone is signed in: the project has
    no entry gates until you add yours. A link is never replayed from Recents
    or after rotation.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  Photo picker, camera and compression
    rememberMediaPicker() for the screen that asks: the system photo picker
    and the camera app, with no permissions to ask for, and one image
    compressor that turns a picked photo upright, scales it and writes a copy
    the app owns.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Play in-app update and review prompts
    A newer build offered once per launch through Play's own sheet and never
    offered again once turned down, and a rating prompt on a schedule Play
    honours. A downloaded update installs when the app next goes to the
    background; the offer to restart into it at once goes to
    LocalMessagePresenter, which logs it until your design system shows
    messages.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Push notifications (FCM)
    Firebase Cloud Messaging alone: notification channels, a tap that opens
    the app, a lock-screen-safe preview, and registration that is renewed only
    when it changes and removed at sign-out. Nothing asks for the notification
    permission yet: rememberNotificationPermission() is ready for the screen
    you build to ask from, and on Android 13 and up notifications stay off
    until it does.
    Requires: firebase
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Analytics and crash-reporting seam
    AnalyticsTracker and CrashReporter interfaces that write nothing until a
    vendor is bound, the settings opt-out applied to both, logged errors kept
    as breadcrumbs, and janky frames counted on the device.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Firebase Crashlytics
    Crashlytics behind the crash-reporting seam: crashes and unhandled errors
    are reported, every logged error is a breadcrumb, and the R8 mapping is
    uploaded only by a release build that asks for it.
    Requires: analytics, firebase
    You left out analytics; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  On-device inspector
    A badge docked to the screen's edge in dev and debug builds, which can be
    dragged out of the way, and a panel behind it with the last 200 requests
    (credentials redacted), their timing and failure rate, and the build's
    details. Absent from a production build rather than hidden in one.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Firebase Analytics
    Firebase Analytics behind the analytics seam, switched off by the
    analytics opt-out in the preference store; the switch that sets it is your
    settings screen's to draw. It brings the advertising-id permissions with
    it, which is why it is a choice of its own.
    Requires: analytics, firebase
    You left out analytics; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Feature flag seam
    Typed flags declared in one file with their defaults beside them, read or
    observed through an interface that resolves locally until a vendor is
    bound, and overridable by hand in the inspector.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Live performance overlay
    Frames per second at the display's real rate and the janky-frame share
    under the DEV badge, and a Performance tab in the inspector: frame times
    against the 60, 90 and 120 Hz budgets, frames per second over time, a
    main-thread time histogram, jank by screen, memory over time, start-up and
    request timings, and an optimisation score from 0 to 100 with a fix for
    every lost point. A structured logcat summary and an exported JSON and
    Markdown report. On in debug builds, a switch away in dev release builds,
    and absent from production builds; while it is off it puts nothing on the
    window. It lives in the on-device inspector, so it is on by default
    wherever the inspector is chosen, as in the Standard preset.
    Requires: devtools
    You left out devtools; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Firebase Remote Config
    Feature flags read from Remote Config: fetched at start-up, updated while
    the app is open when the console publishes a change, and fetched on every
    start in a debug build.
    Requires: flags, firebase
    You left out flags; including this adds it back.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Downloadable Google Font
    The typefaces the spec names (DM Sans and JetBrains Mono unless you choose
    others), downloaded through the Play Services font provider in exactly the
    weights the design style draws with. They are fetched while the splash is
    up; a launch where they take longer than 150 ms draws in the platform font
    throughout, so text never swaps under the reader.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  Continuous integration
    A GitHub Actions workflow that runs `check` (unit tests, lint, the code
    rules, the module graph and the Compose stability gate) and assembles the
    debug and release builds on every push and pull request, uploading the
    reports when something fails.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  Coverage floor (Kover)
    One merged coverage number for the whole project with a floor the build
    enforces, generated code excluded so the report is about code somebody
    wrote.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Dependency health report
    Names every module declaring a dependency it never uses, or using one it
    only gets transitively — the second being the failure that surfaces months
    later as an unrelated module breaking. `./gradlew buildHealth` writes the
    report; it is advice rather than a gate, because two of this template's
    own decisions produce advice it is right to ignore.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  Formatting (Spotless + ktlint)
    Spotless runs ktlint's Android Studio style over every Kotlin and Gradle
    file: `check` fails on a file that is not formatted and `spotlessApply`
    fixes it. The rules live in .editorconfig, which Android Studio reads too.
  Include? [y/N]: 
  LeakCanary (debug only)
    Memory-leak detection in debug builds.
  Include? [Y/n]: 
  Performance harness
    A macrobenchmark module for cold and warm start to the start screen, with
    and without the baseline profile, run against the dev flavour, and the
    baseline profile generator that records the profile every build ships. The
    journey benchmarks and the frame-time scripts come with the components
    they measure.
  Include? [y/N]: 
Architecture
────────────
  1. Compact
     One module per feature and per data source. Simple, and what most apps
     need.
  2. Modular
     Each feature and data module split into :api and :impl, for larger
     teams.

